WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:27.012 --> 00:01:33.536
Life's like a galaxy
floating amongst the sea of stars.

00:01:33.536 --> 00:01:41.297
Black holes try to reverse your course,
You won't let them stop you anyhow.

00:01:41.297 --> 00:01:43.514
Don't be afraid to gather your voice.

00:01:43.514 --> 00:01:48.517
Don't be nervous

00:01:48.517 --> 00:01:56.238
because in this society, 
we won't hesitate to look away.

00:01:56.238 --> 00:02:03.410
Shield of a magnifying glass

00:02:03.410 --> 00:02:10.708
so all they see is half an image

00:02:10.708 --> 00:02:17.958
Oh, take cover,
run down these stairs,

00:02:17.958 --> 00:02:22.197
and run as fast as you can.

00:02:22.197 --> 00:02:25.618
Run as fast as you can.

00:02:25.618 --> 00:02:29.657
Because it's a journey, just you and me.

00:02:29.657 --> 00:02:33.819
Pack your bags and arrividerci.

00:02:33.819 --> 00:02:37.849
Don't let nobody tell you otherwise.

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:56.199
Wait until they see 
the world through your eyes.

00:02:56.199 --> 00:03:02.909
Painting pictures through your lenses
nobody is understanding.

00:03:02.909 --> 00:03:05.489
And from your thoughts
out pours a story

00:03:05.489 --> 00:03:11.279
one would have to learn to read.

00:03:11.279 --> 00:03:17.122
But don't be easily swayed
because your brilliance is seldom seen.

00:03:17.122 --> 00:03:55.422
It's your self-fulfilled prophecy.

00:03:55.422 --> 00:04:04.211
It's not easy 
trapped in a personal mythology.

00:04:04.211 --> 00:04:09.962
If I could look through the glass,
would I ask if I could see what they see?

00:04:09.962 --> 00:04:13.893
But it's a journey, just you and me.

00:04:13.893 --> 00:04:17.802
Pack your bags and arrividerci.

00:04:17.802 --> 00:04:21.742
Don't let nobody tell you otherwise.

00:04:21.742 --> 00:04:36.884
Wait until they see 
the world through your eyes.

00:04:36.884 --> 00:04:39.584
Thank you so much.

00:04:39.584 --> 00:04:41.978
When I was four years old,

00:04:41.978 --> 00:04:44.584
my dad taught me 
the Taos Pueblo hoop dance,

00:04:44.584 --> 00:04:49.526
a traditional dance born hundreds
of years ago in Southwestern USA.

00:04:49.526 --> 00:04:52.322
A series of hoops are constructed
out of willow wood

00:04:52.322 --> 00:04:55.515
and they're used to create formations
of the natural world,

00:04:55.515 --> 00:04:58.245
showing the many beauties of life.

00:04:58.245 --> 00:05:00.654
In the dance, you're circling
in a constant spin,

00:05:00.654 --> 00:05:04.775
mimicking the movement 
of the sun and the passage of time.

00:05:04.775 --> 00:05:08.475
Watching this dance was magic to me.

00:05:08.475 --> 00:05:09.864
Like with a time capsule,

00:05:09.864 --> 00:05:12.665
I was taking a look through 
a cultural window to the past.

00:05:12.665 --> 00:05:17.650
I felt a deeper connection to how my
ancestors looked at the world around them.

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:21.215
And since then, I've always been obsessed
with time capsules.

00:05:21.215 --> 00:05:23.016
They come in many forms,

00:05:23.016 --> 00:05:27.395
but the common thread
is that they hold a portal to a memory,

00:05:27.395 --> 00:05:31.465
and they hold the important power
of keeping stories alive.

00:05:31.465 --> 00:05:35.687
As a filmmaker and composer,
it's been my journey to find my voice,

00:05:35.687 --> 00:05:38.986
reclaim the stories of my heritage
and the past,

00:05:38.986 --> 00:05:41.607
and infuse them together
into music,

00:05:41.607 --> 00:05:42.361
film,

00:05:42.361 --> 00:05:45.747
and virtual reality 
time capsules to share.

00:05:45.747 --> 00:05:48.664
But first, I want to share a bit
about how I grew up.

00:05:48.664 --> 00:05:52.754
In Southern California, I was raised
in a multi-generational home,

00:05:52.754 --> 00:05:54.555
meaning I lived under the same roof

00:05:54.555 --> 00:05:58.587
as my parents, aunts, 
uncles, and grandparents.

00:05:58.587 --> 00:06:02.678
On my mother's side, 
she's Dutch Indonesian and Chinese

00:06:02.678 --> 00:06:04.157
with immigrant parents,

00:06:04.157 --> 00:06:05.901
and my father is Ojibwe

00:06:05.901 --> 00:06:09.358
and an enrolled tribal member
of the prairie band Potawatomi tribe

00:06:09.358 --> 00:06:11.106
in Northeastern Kansas.

00:06:11.106 --> 00:06:13.806
So one weekend, I'd be learning 
how to fold dumplings

00:06:13.806 --> 00:06:17.596
and the next weekend, I'd be powwow 
dancing traditional style

00:06:17.596 --> 00:06:22.088
immersed in the powerful heartbeats
of drums and singers.

00:06:22.088 --> 00:06:25.430
So being surrounded by so many cultures
was the norm for me,

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:28.627
but it was a really confusing experience.

00:06:28.627 --> 00:06:33.138
It was hard for me to find my own voice
because I never felt I was ever enough.

00:06:33.138 --> 00:06:34.539
I was never Chinese enough,

00:06:34.539 --> 00:06:37.170
never Dutch Indonesian
or Native enough,

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:40.755
and because I never felt like I was a part
of any singular community,

00:06:40.755 --> 00:06:43.997
I sought to learn the stories
behind my heritage

00:06:43.997 --> 00:06:47.227
and connect them together
to rediscover my own.

00:06:47.227 --> 00:06:51.019
And the first medium 
I felt gave me a voice was music.

00:06:51.019 --> 00:06:54.639
With layers of sounds 
and multiple instruments,

00:06:54.639 --> 00:06:58.949
I could create soundscapes and worlds
that were much bigger than my own.

00:06:58.949 --> 00:07:02.709
And this right here is actually the setup
that I have in my room.

00:07:02.709 --> 00:07:06.213
This instrument here is one 
of my favorites to play.

00:07:06.213 --> 00:07:08.210
It's the Chinese Guzheng Zither,

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:11.080
and I learned how to play it
from online tutorials,

00:07:11.080 --> 00:07:12.431
and another thing I learned

00:07:12.431 --> 00:07:16.506
was that while the hoop dance 
is hundreds of years old,

00:07:16.506 --> 00:07:21.219
the Chinese Guzheng Zither 
has over 2,000 years of history.

00:07:21.219 --> 00:07:24.131
And I noticed an interesting connection.

00:07:24.131 --> 00:07:26.636
The Zither is tuned 
to the pentatonic scale,

00:07:26.636 --> 00:07:31.279
which is a scale found so universally
within the world,

00:07:31.279 --> 00:07:37.500
and I noticed that native folk songs
also carry with it the pentatonic scale.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:39.855
And in both Chinese and Native Folk,

00:07:39.855 --> 00:07:43.881
I sense the sound of yearning 
and longing for the past

00:07:43.881 --> 00:07:48.585
and this the sound that deeply inspires
the music I create today.

00:07:48.585 --> 00:07:51.360
And at the time, I wondered if I could

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:54.161
make this feeling 
of immersion even more powerful

00:07:54.161 --> 00:07:57.052
by layering images and visuals
over the music.

00:07:57.052 --> 00:08:00.961
So I went back to online tutorials
to learn editing software,

00:08:00.961 --> 00:08:04.051
and went to community college
to save money

00:08:04.051 --> 00:08:05.603
and created films.

00:08:05.603 --> 00:08:07.452
After a couple years of experimenting,

00:08:07.452 --> 00:08:11.312
I was 17 and I had something
that I wanted to tell and preserve,

00:08:11.312 --> 00:08:13.472
and it started with a question.

00:08:13.472 --> 00:08:16.832
What happens when a story is forgotten?

00:08:16.832 --> 00:08:20.832
I lead with this in my latest 
documentary film Smoke that Travels,

00:08:20.832 --> 00:08:25.324
which invites you into a world
of song and dance as I explore my fear

00:08:25.324 --> 00:08:29.782
that a part of my heritage 
will be forgotten in time.

00:08:29.782 --> 00:08:33.524
As of right now, there are so many
Indigenous languages dying

00:08:33.524 --> 00:08:36.653
due to historically forced assimilation.

00:08:36.653 --> 00:08:40.254
From the late 1800s to the early 1970s,

00:08:40.254 --> 00:08:42.735
Natives were forced into boarding schools

00:08:42.735 --> 00:08:46.834
where they were violently punished
if they practiced traditional ways

00:08:46.834 --> 00:08:48.885
or spoke their native language,

00:08:48.885 --> 00:08:51.403
most of which were orally passed down.

00:08:51.403 --> 00:08:56.716
Right now, there are 567 federally 
recognised tribes in the United States

00:08:56.716 --> 00:08:59.504
when there used to be countless more.

00:08:59.504 --> 00:09:04.840
In my father's words, being Native 
is not about the braids

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:07.615
or the feathers and the bead work.

00:09:07.615 --> 00:09:13.523
Being Native is all about how we center
ourselves in the world as human beings.

00:09:13.523 --> 00:09:16.785
After traveling with this film
for over a year,

00:09:16.785 --> 00:09:19.064
I met Indigenous people 
from around the world,

00:09:19.064 --> 00:09:22.635
from the Ainu of Japan
and the Sami of Scandanavia

00:09:22.635 --> 00:09:26.624
and many more who all were dealing
with this exact same struggle,

00:09:26.624 --> 00:09:29.937
to preserve their language and culture.

00:09:29.937 --> 00:09:32.925
And I want to tell a quick story.

00:09:32.925 --> 00:09:34.484
Around that same time,

00:09:34.484 --> 00:09:39.654
I finished my first-ever 
tiny experiment with rotoscoping

00:09:39.654 --> 00:09:41.566
an animation technique.

00:09:41.566 --> 00:09:45.946
I traced over 112 frames from a video

00:09:45.946 --> 00:09:49.026
of a beautiful, fancy shawl dancer
named Danielle Bear,

00:09:49.026 --> 00:09:51.928
and this is how it looked.

00:09:51.928 --> 00:09:55.426
So I posted this online, very tiny video,
not expecting anything,

00:09:55.426 --> 00:09:57.978
just wanting to share it 
with my friends and family.

00:09:57.978 --> 00:10:00.238
And the next morning, unexpectedly,

00:10:00.238 --> 00:10:04.685
I woke up to hundreds and thousands 
of views and comments saying

00:10:04.685 --> 00:10:09.637
"Oh man, I want to bring my shawl
back out and go back to the powwow,"

00:10:09.637 --> 00:10:14.008
or, "Oh, I miss dancing, 
I want to get back out there again."

00:10:14.008 --> 00:10:19.508
And the most striking comment to me
was someone who said,

00:10:19.508 --> 00:10:26.117
"I've never seen a fancy shawl dancer
animated before, ever."

00:10:26.117 --> 00:10:28.838
And prior to this, 
it never even occurred to me

00:10:28.838 --> 00:10:33.096
that there aren't many traditional style
dancers in animated form.

00:10:33.096 --> 00:10:36.069
They just don't exist.

00:10:36.069 --> 00:10:39.436
And at this moment, I not only
realized the power storytelling has

00:10:39.436 --> 00:10:41.680
to connect all of us as human beings,

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:44.718
but the responsibility that comes
with this power.

00:10:44.718 --> 00:10:48.848
It becomes incredibly dangerous
when our stories are rewritten or ignored

00:10:48.848 --> 00:10:54.048
because when we are denied identity,
we become invisible.

00:10:54.048 --> 00:10:58.239
And this concept is so important to me
with new rising mediums,

00:10:58.239 --> 00:10:59.958
like virtual reality.

00:10:59.958 --> 00:11:03.128
In a medium so powerful where you could
surround yourself

00:11:03.128 --> 00:11:05.489
and step into a new world

00:11:05.489 --> 00:11:09.628
and immerse yourself 
into these new perspectives,

00:11:09.628 --> 00:11:13.421
it's so important that we have 
our identities and stories

00:11:13.421 --> 00:11:18.340
existing within them 
in the foundational era right now.

00:11:18.340 --> 00:11:21.341
And it's also important 
for the creators themselves

00:11:21.341 --> 00:11:27.497
to be empowered to make these experiences
just for the joy of storytelling.

00:11:27.497 --> 00:11:29.209
Right now, I'm exploring

00:11:29.209 --> 00:11:35.862
a young VR video game programmer's
cyberpunk musical adventure

00:11:35.862 --> 00:11:38.741
set in Tokyo, Japan.

00:11:38.741 --> 00:11:44.570
And I'm dreaming up this world 
in virtual reality room-scale,

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:48.120
a diary filled with a treasure trove
of objects,

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:50.967
from a favorite toy 
to an old family trinket,

00:11:50.967 --> 00:11:53.500
3-D scanned in from the real world,

00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:55.292
and when you pick up that object

00:11:55.292 --> 00:11:58.358
you could hear the very story
behind it,

00:11:58.358 --> 00:12:02.331
just like a time capsule,
again, like a window to the past.

00:12:02.331 --> 00:12:08.133
And I'm more fascinated with creating
alternative realities

00:12:08.133 --> 00:12:15.060
that let us draw parallels to our own,
rather than recreating the real world.

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:18.553
Because all around us here,
we're all mixed,

00:12:18.553 --> 00:12:22.832
mixed backgrounds, mixed cultures,
different hometowns,

00:12:22.832 --> 00:12:27.173
and whether we know it or not,
whether we're aware of it or not,

00:12:27.173 --> 00:12:31.032
we have so many stories and events
in our lives and our histories

00:12:31.032 --> 00:12:33.573
that make up who we are today.

00:12:33.573 --> 00:12:36.132
And we're all storytellers.

00:12:36.132 --> 00:12:41.923
Perhaps reclaiming our narratives
and just listening to each other's

00:12:41.923 --> 00:12:45.851
can create a portal 
that can transcend time itself.

